{"attempt_id": "attempt:um-guy-a14a-pomerance-s-questions-on-good-primes-c24759ec-source-fidelity-01", "created_at": "2026-09-06T15:37:58Z", "graph_id": "graph:um-guy-a14a-pomerance-s-questions-on-good-primes-c24759ec-v1", "obligations": [{"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof", "counterexample", "formalization", "computation"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": ["obligation:um-guy-a14a-pomerance-s-questions-on-good-primes-c24759ec-statement-fidelity"], "kind": "root_claim", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-guy-a14a-pomerance-s-questions-on-good-primes-c24759ec-root", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/GUY-A14a"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Call prime $p_n$ good if $p_n^2 > p_{n-i}p_{n+i}$ for all $i$, $1 \\le i \\le n-1$. Is it true that the set of $n$ for which $p_n$ is good has density 0? Are there infinitely many $n$ with $p_n p_{n+1} > p_{n-i} p_{n+1+i}$ for all $i$, $1 \\le i \\le n-1$?"}, "statement_sha256": "fe9baf1bbdba800a17a78c2e8afc0a7c8ba8747b869e4c5705f1ddaabba92bfd"}, {"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": [], "kind": "definition", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-guy-a14a-pomerance-s-questions-on-good-primes-c24759ec-statement-fidelity", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/GUY-A14a"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Verify against the cited source that the frozen statement is complete, current, untruncated, attribution-correct, and unambiguous enough for mathematical research; record any definition or quantifier gap without silently editing the contract."}, "statement_sha256": "65f0b3d574bd6d837403149dab6434c0f571709f7eb5bb066c7e8774bad13f34"}], "problem_contract_sha256": "348adbf043ab67529ab903087babeac41e8e7d61f188333fa4b3efbffecf3b69", "problem_id": "problem:um-guy-a14a-p